# Analyze > CFD table end to end: choosing the method starts a real solve (the example's ten Mach numbers, 0.2 to 3, coarse grid,
# on the GPU through Luma). The headless driver waits for every running task before the next step, so the click on
# "CFD table" returns once the solve has finished and the table is stored in the document (minutes; see `cfd.rs`).
#   cargo run -q -p bowshock -- drive --example crates/bowshock/scripts/cfd.drive out/cfd
# With `BOWSHOCK_OPEN=<file.bowshock>` naming a document that already carries the table (made by the ignored test
# `the_example_table_solves_and_agrees_with_extended` with `BOWSHOCK_CFD_FIXTURE=<file>`), nothing is solved again and the
# provenance reads `… · solved on GPU (Luma) · N min ago`.
viewport 1300x800
click "Analyze"
click "CFD table"
move (100, 700)
move (102, 702)
screenshot cfd-dark.png

# The drag stack and the table view.
click "Drag breakdown"
move (500, 300)
move (502, 302)
screenshot cfd-drag-dark.png
click "Aerodynamics"
click "Table view"
screenshot cfd-table-dark.png
click "Plots"

# CFD against Extended on the same plots.
click "Compare ▾"
click "Compare with Extended"
move (700, 300)
move (702, 302)
screenshot cfd-vs-extended-dark.png

# The light theme.
click "Prefs"
click "Light"
click "Done"
move (700, 300)
move (702, 302)
screenshot cfd-vs-extended-light.png
click "Compare ▾"
click "Clear comparison"
move (100, 700)
move (102, 702)
screenshot cfd-light.png
